## heavenzip links nothing

This is the point of the repository. It chains prebuilt binaries through a process
boundary and compiles no third-party code into itself — `CMakeLists.txt` links
`heavenzip_core` and the C++ standard library, and nothing else. Every tool it
spawns keeps its own licence, and spawning is not linking.

That is why the fleet has two repos instead of one: `ziptastic` links, and
therefore carries other people's terms in the artefact it ships; `heavenzip`
spawns, and does not.

## What a release bundle contains, and under what terms

The release tarball carries every tool a profile can name, so a fresh install does
not refuse at pre-flight. Each one is a separate program in `bin/`, spawned and
never linked, and each keeps its own terms:

| Tool | Licence | What travels with it |
|---|---|---|
| `ziptastic` | MIT (same fleet) | the binary, verified against its release `SHA256SUMS` |
| `paq8px` | **GPL-2.0-or-later** | the binary **and its source** (`sources/paq8px-source.tar.gz`) — GPL-2 §3 requires it |
| `arc` + `unarc` | **GPL-2.0** | the binaries **and their source** — GPL-2 §3 requires it |
| `7z` | **LGPL-2.1** with the unRAR restriction on the Rar* files | the binary **and its source** |
| `xz` | **0BSD** (the `xz` tool; liblzma is 0BSD too) | the binary **and its source** |
| `precomp` | **Apache-2.0** | the binary **and its source** |
| `srep` | **the author's, not ours** — see below | the binary **and its source** (`sources/srep-source.tar.gz`) plus the build recipe; see below |
| `brotli` | MIT | the binary, built statically from the vault so it needs only libc and libm |
| `preflate` | Apache-2.0 | **not in this bundle**: it does not compile on POSIX. A Windows build ships separately as `preflate-windows-x86_64.tar.gz`, and the engine already links preflate internally |

### `srep` has no licence, and this is what that means

`srep` (SuperREP 3.93a) is the private project of **Bulat Ziganshin**. The licence is
his to grant, and **we do not hold it**: there is no licence file and no licence
statement anywhere in that tree. Absence of a licence is not permission — by default
the author reserves all rights — so nothing here grants you any right to `srep`,
and nothing in this repository implies we were given one.

It is included as an unofficial repackage of the last known commit, with its source
(`sources/srep-source.tar.gz`) and the build recipe in `docs/PACKAGING.md`, so that
what is shipped can be inspected and rebuilt rather than taken on trust. If the
author asks for it to be removed, it will be removed.

If you are packaging heavenzip for other people, that is the decision to review
before you publish — including the binary is a choice the publisher makes, not one
this repository can make for them.

`srep` is optional. Every profile that names it refuses, naming the program, when
it is absent; nothing else depends on it.

### `preflate` is Apache-2.0 and does not build here

Its vendored `support/filestream.cpp` calls `_ftelli64` and `_fseeki64`
unconditionally — MSVC-only names with no `#ifdef` — so it does not compile with
gcc or clang on Linux. No binary is shipped. Its licence text is included for the
day it does.

## What is in this repository that heavenzip did not write

`bin/` holds one compiled binary, committed by the `sync-binaries` workflow:

| File | What it is | Licence |
|---|---|---|
| `bin/ziptastic-1.4.2-linux-x86_64` | the CHCE engine, built and released by `drsamonline/ziptastic` | MIT (same fleet) |
| `bin/ENGINE` | a four-line record of the tag, asset, SHA-256 and fetch date | — |

That binary contains third-party code — xz/liblzma, preflate, libbsc, libzpaq,
7-Zip PPMd and precomp-cpp contrib — because `ziptastic` links them. Their
licences travel with it: see `ziptastic`'s `NOTICE` and HCS's `LICENSES/`.

A released build may also carry tools the user would otherwise install
themselves. `docs/PACKAGING.md` records the decision, and `docs/TOOL_MANIFEST.md`
carries the per-tool verdict:

- **may ship**: `preflate` (Apache-2.0), `paq8px` (GPL-2+, as a separate program
  with its source), `ECT` (Apache-2.0);
- **may not ship**: `srep` — "All rights reserved" with no licence file, so not
  redistributable in any form — and every commercial or unlicensed tool on the
  shelf.

A default release contains `ziptastic` and nothing else.

## Two things a reader should know

- **`bin/` is not a licence-free zone.** The `.gitignore` here lists
  `bin/ziptastic`, `bin/srep`, `bin/paq8px` and `bin/preflate` — but the file
  actually committed is `ziptastic-1.4.2-linux-x86_64`, which none of those
  patterns match. The binary is in git, deliberately (that is what
  `sync-binaries` commits), and `docs/TOOL_MANIFEST.md`'s "`bin/` stays empty in
  the repository" describes an intention this repository does not follow. Both
  statements should be reconciled; this file describes what is, not what was
  intended.
- **Offline at runtime.** Nothing in heavenzip reaches the network. The
  `sync-binaries` workflow does, at build time, and is the only thing that does.

